[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. The reports in it tell
three stories. Fixing the parser closes one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket, and only one of them is the parser.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** Every tenant on file writes the
day first, so their dates come out with day and month swapped: booked 2 March, invited for
3 February. This is the bug the queued fix is for.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form asks for the wrong order.** Its date field prints `MM/DD/YYYY`
under it. People type what the hint says, and the form reads the date the tenant's way, day
first. The symptom looks the same as `parser`, so intake cannot tell them apart. The parser fix
does not touch it: it only happens on the `form` channel of booking-v3 tenants, and it needs
the hint (or the form's reading) changed.

**`one-day`: the date moves by one day.** Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May; a client in
Lisbon gets Tuesday and a client in Toronto gets Monday for the same meeting; an invite arrives
before the booking. Nothing is swapped. The desk noted these reports by hand
(`desk-notes.json`) and still counted them under `parser`. They point at time zones, not at
date order, and the parser fix will not close them.

## How they were counted

- One report, one id: the ticket export repeats some reports across pages, so duplicates are
  dropped before counting.
- By tenant and channel, not by intake's tag: intake guesses from the reporter's browser and is
  wrong often enough that the tag cannot be trusted as a count.
- Not by subject: the same subject line covers different problems
  ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone; that is how
  all three ended up in one ticket).
- Every number on this page is computed from the export when the page renders, so it follows
  the data as the desk keeps working.

